**Theft Detection Lock is a free Android setting that locks your screen when the phone senses that someone grabbed it out of your hand and ran, biked or drove away.** Turn it on under `Settings > Google > All services > Theft protection`. It protects what's on the phone, but it doesn't help you get the phone back, and it never shows you who took it. Google also lists situations where it may not trigger at all.

## What Theft Detection Lock does

Most phone thefts in the street happen while the phone is unlocked and in your hand. That's the worst moment to lose it: the thief has an open phone with your email, photos and banking apps.

Theft Detection Lock is built for that moment. Google says it uses AI, your phone's motion sensors, Wi-Fi and Bluetooth to detect when someone unexpectedly takes the phone and runs, bikes or drives away. When it does, it locks the screen right away. The thief is left with a locked phone and needs your PIN, pattern or password to get any further.

It works on the phone itself. You don't have to do anything when it happens.

## How to turn on Theft Detection Lock

You need a screen lock (PIN, pattern or password) first.

**Go to:**
`Settings > Google > All services > Theft protection`

Turn on **Theft Detection Lock**. While you're there, turn on the two settings next to it as well:

- **Offline Device Lock** locks the screen after the phone has been used offline for a short time, for example when a thief switches on airplane mode. The phone has to be unlocked when it loses its connection, and it can lock this way twice in 24 hours.
- **Failed Authentication Lock** locks the screen when unlock attempts fail repeatedly. It's on by default.

On a Pixel, the same menu is also under `Settings > Security & privacy > Device unlock > Theft protection`. On a Samsung Galaxy, look under `Settings > Security and privacy > Lost device protection > Theft protection`.

If the switch is grayed out, your phone doesn't support the feature. Google says these features aren't supported on tablets, wearables or Android Go phones, and that support can vary by phone model. They need Android 10 or later, and some need Android 15 or later.

On new Android phones activated in Brazil, Google turns Theft Detection Lock and Remote Lock on by default. Everywhere else, check it yourself.

## When Theft Detection Lock may not trigger

Google is open about this on its help page. To avoid locking your phone during normal use, Theft Detection Lock may not trigger when:

- **Your phone has a stable Wi-Fi or Bluetooth connection.** At home, at work, or with your earbuds or watch connected and nearby, it may stay quiet.
- **It has already locked the phone several times in a short period.**

It also does nothing when the screen is already locked, because there's nothing left to lock.

And it only recognizes one kind of theft: a sudden grab followed by someone moving away fast. It won't react to:

- a phone lifted quietly from a pocket, bag or table,
- a phone you left behind in a taxi or a bar,
- someone at home or at work picking up your phone and trying your PIN.

If it locks your phone by mistake, for example when you run for a bus, just unlock it and carry on.

## What Theft Detection Lock can't do

Even when it works perfectly, the result is a locked phone in someone else's hands. Theft Detection Lock doesn't:

- **Show you who took it.** No photo of the person.
- **Tell you it happened.** There's no alert to your email or another device.
- **Tell you where the phone is.** Finding it is a separate feature, Find Hub.
- **Stop the thief from turning the phone off.** See [how to stop power off and airplane mode from the lock screen](/blog/block-power-menu).

For the phone itself, pair it with the other built-in settings:

- **Find Hub** shows the phone on a map and lets you ring, lock or erase it.
- **Remote Lock** lets you lock the phone from any browser at [android.com/lock](https://www.android.com/lock) with just your phone number. It needs an active SIM, a verified phone number, Find Hub turned on and an internet connection.

All five are explained in [5 Android anti-theft settings to turn on today](/blog/top_five_settings).

## How to get a photo of the person who has your phone

After Theft Detection Lock has done its job, the thief is looking at your lock screen. Almost every thief then does the same thing: tries a few PINs.

That's the moment CrookCatcher uses. Android notifies apps with the **Device admin** permission each time an unlock attempt fails. CrookCatcher then takes a photo with the front camera, records the GPS location and an approximate address, and emails both to you. The photo and the location are in your inbox even if the phone is switched off a minute later, and you can give them to the police.

