## Frequently Asked Questions

### Q: Is Theft Detection Lock on by default?
A: Usually not. Google turns it on by default on new Android phones activated in Brazil. On other phones, check under `Settings > Google > All services > Theft protection`.

### Q: Which phones have Theft Detection Lock?
A: Most Android phones with Google services, running Android 10 or later. Google says it isn't supported on tablets, wearables or Android Go phones, and that support can vary by model. If the switch is grayed out, your phone doesn't support it.

### Q: Why didn't Theft Detection Lock lock my phone?
A: Google says it may not trigger when the phone has a stable Wi-Fi or Bluetooth connection, or after several locks in a short period. It also only reacts to a grab followed by fast movement, and it isn't active when the screen is already locked.

### Q: How do I turn off Theft Detection Lock?
A: Go to `Settings > Google > All services > Theft protection` and turn off the Theft Detection Lock switch. If it locks your phone by mistake now and then, you only need to unlock it again, so most people are better off leaving it on.

### Q: Does Theft Detection Lock track my phone?
A: No. It only locks the screen. To see where the phone is, use Find Hub at [google.com/android/find](https://www.google.com/android/find).

### Q: Does Theft Detection Lock take a photo of the thief?
A: No. Android's theft protection never uses the camera. To get a photo and the location of anyone who then tries to unlock the phone, you need an app like CrookCatcher.

